#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068865 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36e2e343-4403-51b2-965f-168bbe606112" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Combination of Horizontal and Vertical Translations"}] :question/id #uuid "9c2447fb-d21a-553f-bc38-243ef63696ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-1445" :question/problem "Identify the equation of the graph shown below. \n\n![](images/36/36a2a45f6078cdf04b798c39b6382c1f40411375a3f16ef77e996b39aa0009b3.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the graph of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is a translation of the standard parabola $y=x^2$ by\n\n- $h$ units right, and\n\n- $k$ units up.\n\nThe vertex of $y=(x-h)^2+k$ is located at $(h,k).$\n\nHere, the parabola is translated $2$ units *left*, so we have $h=-2.$ The parabola is also translated $1$ unit up, so we have $k=1.$ We can also see that the vertex is at $(-2,1),$ so again we must have $h=-2$ and $k=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the graph is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-(-2))^{2}+1 \\\\ y & =(x+2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068525 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd6345-30ba-5ecf-8123-8e8d624e794f" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Vertical Translation of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "8106f0ee-a91b-5535-bc05-fe874dc661f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-1793" :question/problem "Identify the equation that generated the graph below.\n\n![](images/90/9051b7b34f3b88cbc9b08e0be2ae36b955a081f9cb81a85f667aa6076e3e1859.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in general, the graph of $y=x^2+k$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $k$ units up. The vertex of $y=x^2+k$ is located at $(0,k).$\n\nHere, the curve shown is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units upward. The vertex is located at $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, $k=3,$ and the equation of the parabola is $y = x^2 +3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac1b2aa5-e238-55c5-a515-7b688bc5a960" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Horizontal Translation of a Parabola"}] :question/id #uuid "a301e57f-0f6f-5078-a2fb-7c39fef91f3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1794" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of the function $y=(x+3)^2.$ What are the coordinates of the minimum point?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the graph of $y=(x-h)^2$ looks like the standard parabola $y=x^2$ but translated $h$ units right. The vertex of $y=(x-h)^2$ is located at $(h,0).$\n\nIn the given function $y=(x+3)^2,$ we have $h=-3.$ To see this clearly, we can write the curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x+3)^{2} \\\\ y & =(x-(-3))^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the parabola $y=x^2$ after being translated $3$ units *left*. \n\n![](images/bf/bffdacd979eaa77a7a64364840ab318bcc4a1c39ca78137146e82f37540e02e4.png)\n\nThe vertex is located at $(-3,0),$ and these are the coordinates of the minimum point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac1b2aa5-e238-55c5-a515-7b688bc5a960" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Horizontal Translation of a Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "754e42d5-00b8-5220-ac8f-4211ab364e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528568} {:answer/id #uuid "c440783e-0d16-53b5-9063-c5d7f787f510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528779} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc8e79-ffa4-5828-8957-7b048c0b06c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528782} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6f426e-8e62-5aa5-bc0d-861124834bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218